The biggest mistake people make while learning LLM fine-tuning?
They mix together things that belong in completely different categories.

Someone says:
"Should I use SFT or LoRA?"
That question doesn't even make sense.
Because SFT and LoRA solve different problems.

Here's the mental model that simplified fine-tuning for me:
Fine-tuning has 3 separate layers.

Layer 1 → What data are you training on?
↳ Instruction data
↳ Chat conversations
↳ Preference data

Layer 2 → How does the model learn?
↳ SFT
↳ RLHF
↳ DPO
↳ PPO

Layer 3 → How much of the model changes?
↳ Full Fine-Tuning
↳ LoRA
↳ QLoRA
↳ Adapters

Most confusion happens when these layers get mixed together.

For example:
SFT is a learning method.
LoRA is a parameter update method.
RLHF is a preference learning method.

Instruction tuning is simply a type of dataset.
They're not competing techniques.
They're different pieces of the same pipeline.

A real training workflow often looks like:
Pretraining
↓
SFT
↓
RLHF / DPO
↓
Deployment

And each stage can use:
↳ Full Fine-Tuning
or
↳ LoRA / QLoRA

Once you separate:
↳ Data
↳ Learning
↳ Parameters

The entire fine-tuning ecosystem becomes much easier to understand.
That's the framework I wish someone had shown me when I started learning LLMs.





















🔹 1. Tokens
LLMs don't read words.
They read tokens.
A token can be:
• A word
• Part of a word
• A punctuation mark
The more tokens you use, the more processing power and cost are involved.

🔹 2. Context Window
This is the model's working memory.
It determines how much information the model can consider at one time.
A larger context window means:
✅ Better long conversations
✅ Larger documents
✅ More accurate reasoning

🔹 3. Embeddings
Embeddings convert text into numbers.
Why?
Because computers understand math, not language.
Embeddings help AI understand that:
"Car"
"Vehicle"
"Automobile"
are closely related concepts.
This is the foundation of semantic search and RAG.

🔹 4. RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)
One of the biggest misconceptions:
LLMs do not automatically know your company data.
RAG allows AI to:
→ Retrieve relevant information
→ Feed it to the model
→ Generate grounded answers
Without RAG, your AI is guessing.
With RAG, your AI is referencing.

🔹 5. Inference
Training teaches the model.
Inference is when the model actually performs a task.
Every time you ask ChatGPT a question, you're running inference.
That's where speed, latency, and cost matter.
